+// ===================== S2Arm (fork): shared tiles (off unless S2A_VT_SHARE_HEAP=1) =====================
+// The terrain arrays of Mario Kart World are Metal placement sparse textures with 64 KB pages (as nxnative's host makes its sparse textures) whose tiles come from shared placement heaps. A tile is a slot; the slot
+//   of a 64 KB chunk is keyed by the physical memory behind it and the texture format, so every place the game maps that memory at (the game maps one physical page at about three places) maps the same slot: a write
+//   through one place, whether a copy, a draw or a compute store, reads at the others, as on the console. Mapping goes through the Metal 4 queue (updateTextureMappings with a heap offset), which the vt-probe showed to work
+//   here. The 16 KB tiles of the old classic path (sparse_map_tiles) gave every texture its own heap, so the arrays the game samples held none of what it wrote through the others.
+static NSHashTable *g_sp_all;   // (declared again where it is defined below)
+#define VS_HEAP_TILES 4096u   // a 256 MB heap of 64 KB tiles
+enum { VS_HN = 1 << 18 };
+typedef struct { uintptr_t key; uint32_t fmt, rc; } vs_tab_t;
